WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.778
Alginate is a sugar from seaweed. On its own it is

2
00:00:02.878 --> 00:00:05.945
just a floppy chain, but add calcium, and it snaps into

3
00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:08.651
a gel. The way it does that has a lovely shape.

4
00:00:09.451 --> 00:00:12.827
Certain stretches of the chain, the G-blocks, are buckled,

5
00:00:12.927 --> 00:00:15.045
and two of them line up face to face.

6
00:00:16.112 --> 00:00:18.817
Between them sit calcium ions, each cradled by the

7
00:00:18.917 --> 00:00:21.790
buckles above and below, exactly like eggs nestled in

8
00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.763
an egg carton. Those junctions lock the gel together.

9
00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.289
Repeat that junction many times over and the floppy solution

10
00:00:30.389 --> 00:00:33.712
becomes a springy, load-bearing solid that grips the sand grains.

11
00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:37.133
There is an honest catch. Alginate dissolves in water, so every

12
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:39.956
rain shower washes a little of the gel away. That is

13
00:00:40.056 --> 00:00:42.725
why it is one route of three, not the whole answer.

